# Release notes stay generic

For **this entire HomeWlanTrainer / steadyGrind (steadygrind) project**, user-facing release notes and public docs must not include personal athlete configuration or habits.

## Do not put in `WHAT_IS_NEW.md`, GitHub Release notes, README highlights, or marketing copy

- Concrete weekly volumes the maintainer (or any named user) prefers — e.g. “5 bike + 4 run”, “10 hours/week”, specific goal notes, or schedule preferences (“runs in the morning”)
- Personal API keys, emails, Garmin usernames, device IDs, FTP values, or local paths tied to one person
- Sample plan parameters that read as *the* product default when they are really one athlete’s settings

## Prefer

- Describe capability: “same-day doubles honor high requested day counts”
- Describe UI/API fields abstractly: “weeks / hours / bike·run days / goal / notes”
- Use fictional or clearly illustrative placeholders only when an example is required for clarity — never copy real user settings from Settings, Plan form, or chat

## When writing or regenerating dist notes

1. Scrub `dist/latest/WHAT_IS_NEW.md` and any template in `deploy/macos/publish_dmg.sh` (or similar) before shipping.
2. Keep `CHANGELOG.md` technical; avoid framing bugfixes around a specific athlete’s week plan numbers unless restated generically.
3. If chat or a ticket includes personal volumes, generalize before committing docs.
